1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,251
A fish in the college pond.

2
00:00:02,251 --> 00:00:04,052
A tadpole in a rain puddle.

3
00:00:04,052 --> 00:00:06,560
You, a few weeks before you were born.

4
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:08,394
Very different animals.

5
00:00:08,394 --> 00:00:12,668
But early in life, all three had a
stiff rod along the back, a tube of

6
00:00:12,668 --> 00:00:15,281
nerves above it, and slits in the throat.

7
00:00:15,731 --> 00:00:20,505
Diya saw this in her textbook: a fish
embryo and a human embryo, side by

8
00:00:20,505 --> 00:00:21,405
side.

9
00:00:21,067 --> 00:00:23,861
She asked, why do we look like cousins?

10
00:00:24,211 --> 00:00:26,199
Because both are chordates.

11
00:00:26,199 --> 00:00:30,186
Those shared features are
exactly how biologists spot one.

12
00:00:30,686 --> 00:00:35,147
By the end, you will know the three
features of every chordate, how chordates

13
00:00:35,147 --> 00:00:38,948
differ from all other animals,
and the three groups inside them.

14
00:00:39,598 --> 00:00:43,885
Here is the body plan that every
chordate shows, at least at some stage

15
00:00:43,885 --> 00:00:44,858
of its life.

16
00:00:45,208 --> 00:00:47,023
Feature one: the notochord.

17
00:00:47,023 --> 00:00:49,438
A stiff but bendy rod along the back.

18
00:00:49,438 --> 00:00:53,051
It holds the body straight,
like the stick inside a kite.

19
00:00:53,551 --> 00:00:56,366
Feature two: the dorsal hollow nerve cord.

20
00:00:56,366 --> 00:00:58,436
Dorsal means on the back side.

21
00:00:58,436 --> 00:01:02,329
It is a hollow tube, and it
lies just above the notochord.

22
00:01:02,829 --> 00:01:05,670
Feature three: paired
pharyngeal gill slits.

23
00:01:05,670 --> 00:01:07,476
The pharynx is the throat.

24
00:01:07,476 --> 00:01:10,833
Its walls have openings,
in pairs, on both sides.

25
00:01:11,333 --> 00:01:16,117
Chordates also have a tail behind the
anus, called a post anal tail, and

26
00:01:16,117 --> 00:01:18,045
a closed circulatory system.

27
00:01:18,545 --> 00:01:21,538
Now cut the animal across,
and look at the slice.

28
00:01:21,888 --> 00:01:25,613
On top sits the nerve cord, a
tube with a hole in the middle.

29
00:01:25,613 --> 00:01:27,239
That is what hollow means.

30
00:01:27,739 --> 00:01:31,117
Below it lies the notochord,
and below that, the gut.

31
00:01:31,117 --> 00:01:33,144
The heart is on the belly side.

32
00:01:33,144 --> 00:01:34,550
We call that ventral.

33
00:01:35,050 --> 00:01:36,285
One more thing.

34
00:01:36,285 --> 00:01:40,814
Chordates are bilaterally symmetrical,
triploblastic and coelomate, with

35
00:01:40,814 --> 00:01:43,439
an organ system level of organisation.

36
00:01:44,089 --> 00:01:46,326
Phylum Chordata has three subphyla.

37
00:01:46,326 --> 00:01:50,526
Watch what happens to the notochord
in each one, over a lifetime.

38
00:01:50,876 --> 00:01:51,776
Step one.

39
00:01:51,761 --> 00:01:52,743
Urochordata.

40
00:01:52,743 --> 00:01:57,027
Here the notochord is present
only in the tail of the larva.

41
00:01:57,027 --> 00:01:58,581
The adult loses it.

42
00:01:58,581 --> 00:02:00,005
Example: Ascidia.

43
00:02:00,755 --> 00:02:01,655
Step two.

44
00:02:01,589 --> 00:02:02,719
Cephalochordata.

45
00:02:02,719 --> 00:02:07,592
Here the notochord runs from head to
tail, and stays for the whole life.

46
00:02:07,592 --> 00:02:10,909
Example: Branchiostoma,
also called the lancelet.

47
00:02:11,659 --> 00:02:12,622
Step three.

48
00:02:12,622 --> 00:02:13,523
Vertebrata.

49
00:02:13,523 --> 00:02:16,591
The notochord is present
only in the embryo.

50
00:02:16,591 --> 00:02:21,335
In the adult, a vertebral column
takes its place, made of cartilage or

51
00:02:21,335 --> 00:02:22,235
bone.

52
00:02:22,671 --> 00:02:25,511
These first two groups
are called protochordates.

53
00:02:25,511 --> 00:02:27,204
They all live in the sea.

54
00:02:27,854 --> 00:02:29,254
Vertebrates add more.

55
00:02:29,254 --> 00:02:33,680
A muscular heart on the belly side,
with two, three or four chambers.

56
00:02:33,680 --> 00:02:36,442
Kidneys, to remove
waste and balance water.

57
00:02:36,892 --> 00:02:41,267
And paired appendages: fins in a
fish, limbs in a frog or in you.

58
00:02:41,267 --> 00:02:45,855
So, all vertebrates are chordates,
but all chordates are not vertebrates.

59
00:02:46,505 --> 00:02:49,199
Now compare a chordate
with a non chordate.

60
00:02:49,199 --> 00:02:50,929
A fish, and a cockroach.

61
00:02:51,279 --> 00:02:53,055
The fish has a notochord.

62
00:02:53,055 --> 00:02:54,673
The cockroach has none.

63
00:02:55,123 --> 00:02:58,421
The fish's nerve cord is
dorsal, hollow and single.

64
00:02:58,421 --> 00:03:01,311
The cockroach's is
ventral, solid and double.

65
00:03:01,761 --> 00:03:03,979
The fish's throat has gill slits.

66
00:03:03,979 --> 00:03:06,197
The cockroach has no gill slits.

67
00:03:06,647 --> 00:03:10,674
The fish's heart is ventral, and
it has a tail after the anus.

68
00:03:10,674 --> 00:03:14,739
The cockroach's heart is dorsal,
and there is no post anal tail.

69
00:03:15,389 --> 00:03:16,306
Your turn.

70
00:03:16,306 --> 00:03:20,048
An earthworm has a nerve
cord running along its belly.

71
00:03:20,048 --> 00:03:21,344
Chordate or not?

72
00:03:21,344 --> 00:03:22,693
Pause and decide.

73
00:03:25,893 --> 00:03:27,101
Not a chordate!

74
00:03:27,101 --> 00:03:30,098
A chordate's nerve cord
is dorsal and hollow.

75
00:03:30,098 --> 00:03:32,564
The earthworm's is ventral and solid.

76
00:03:33,214 --> 00:03:34,295
Remember this.

77
00:03:34,295 --> 00:03:36,316
A notochord makes a chordate.

78
00:03:36,316 --> 00:03:38,387
A backbone makes a vertebrate.

79
00:03:38,987 --> 00:03:40,681
Now, a worked example.

80
00:03:40,681 --> 00:03:42,584
Let's sort four sea animals.

81
00:03:42,934 --> 00:03:43,834
Ascidia.

82
00:03:43,684 --> 00:03:47,220
Its larva has a notochord,
but only in the tail.

83
00:03:47,220 --> 00:03:49,015
So it is a urochordate.

84
00:03:49,465 --> 00:03:50,577
Branchiostoma.

85
00:03:50,577 --> 00:03:53,802
Its notochord runs head to tail, for life.

86
00:03:53,802 --> 00:03:55,200
A cephalochordate.

87
00:03:55,650 --> 00:03:57,345
Labeo, the rohu fish.

88
00:03:57,345 --> 00:04:01,872
Its embryo has a notochord, and
the adult has a backbone of bone.

89
00:04:01,872 --> 00:04:02,927
A vertebrate.

90
00:04:03,377 --> 00:04:04,451
Balanoglossus.

91
00:04:04,451 --> 00:04:07,567
It has a stomochord, not a true notochord.

92
00:04:07,567 --> 00:04:10,023
Once it was placed under Chordata.

93
00:04:10,023 --> 00:04:13,788
Now Hemichordata is a separate
phylum of non chordates.

94
00:04:14,438 --> 00:04:15,904
Now, the exam trap.

95
00:04:16,254 --> 00:04:19,086
Many students say every
chordate has a backbone.

96
00:04:19,486 --> 00:04:24,089
But urochordates and cephalochordates
never form a vertebral column.

97
00:04:24,089 --> 00:04:27,912
Only vertebrates replace the
notochord with a backbone.

98
00:04:28,562 --> 00:04:30,005
Where is this asked?

99
00:04:30,005 --> 00:04:34,429
As statements on the notochord in each
subphylum, and one line differences

100
00:04:34,429 --> 00:04:36,221
from table four point one.

101
00:04:36,821 --> 00:04:38,039
Exam level now.

102
00:04:38,039 --> 00:04:39,835
Read the four statements.

103
00:04:39,835 --> 00:04:41,528
Which ones are correct?

104
00:04:41,528 --> 00:04:42,797
Pause and solve.

105
00:04:48,197 --> 00:04:49,466
The answer is B.

106
00:04:49,466 --> 00:04:51,532
Statements one, two and four.

107
00:04:52,032 --> 00:04:53,191
Three is wrong.

108
00:04:53,191 --> 00:04:56,215
That nerve cord is
ventral, solid and double.

109
00:04:56,215 --> 00:04:58,119
That describes non chordates.

110
00:04:58,619 --> 00:05:03,045
One and two are the notochord rules
for the two protochordate groups.

111
00:05:03,045 --> 00:05:05,818
Four is the current place of Hemichordata.

112
00:05:06,418 --> 00:05:07,877
Here is your recap.

113
00:05:07,877 --> 00:05:09,376
Screenshot this card.

114
00:05:12,576 --> 00:05:15,413
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

115
00:05:15,813 --> 00:05:16,713
One.

116
00:05:16,315 --> 00:05:19,750
What are the three fundamental
features of a chordate?

117
00:05:20,650 --> 00:05:21,550
Two.

118
00:05:21,117 --> 00:05:24,568
Where is the nerve cord in
a fish, and in a cockroach?

119
00:05:25,468 --> 00:05:26,368
Three.

120
00:05:26,066 --> 00:05:29,581
Why is Branchiostoma a
chordate, but not a vertebrate?

121
00:05:30,681 --> 00:05:34,895
Next time: a frog, a lizard, a
crow and a cat near the hostel.

122
00:05:34,895 --> 00:05:37,458
What puts each one in a different class?

123
00:05:37,458 --> 00:05:39,579
We find out in vertebrate groups.
